00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:58.000
Flashman, you may recall, called him a murderous tyrant shrieking with hysterical glee. And Alan Moorhead, who wrote two brilliant books on the history of the Nile, said he has a reputation in the historiography of a mad dog let loose, a sort of black reincarnation of Ivan the terrible. But I think this is very unfair. because actually when Plowton first visited this guy's camp, Teodros liked to spend a lot of time on campaign, so he was always intense. He visits his camp in summer 1855, and he's actually very impressed by Teodros.
弗莱什曼，你可能还记得，称他为“发出歇斯底里狂笑的杀人暴君”。而写了两本关于尼罗河历史的杰出著作的艾伦·穆尔黑德说，他在史学界的名声是一个被放出来的疯狗，一种伊凡雷帝的黑色转世。但我认为这非常不公平。因为实际上当普劳登第一次访问这个家伙的营地时，提奥多罗斯喜欢花很多时间在战场上，所以他总是很紧张。他在1855年夏天访问了他的营地，实际上对提奥多罗斯印象非常深刻。

27
00:13:58.000 --> 00:14:29.000
He finds him kind of reclining on a divan, surrounded by musicians playing flutes and banging drums. So very kind of, this is absolute, you know, European meeting Indigenous king bingo. Reclining on a divan is pretty much obligatory, isn't it? Obligatory, exactly. And actually in his report to the Foreign Office, he said this guy is very charming and very impressive. He's young, he's vigorous, he's polite and engaging when pleased, and mostly displaying great tact and delicacy.
他发现他躺在沙发上，周围是吹着笛子、敲着鼓的音乐家。所以非常，你知道，这是典型的“欧洲人遇见土著国王”的桥段。躺在沙发上几乎是必须的，不是吗？必须的，没错。实际上在他给外交部的报告中，他说这个家伙非常迷人，非常令人印象深刻。他年轻、精力充沛、礼貌且引人入胜，当他高兴时，大多表现出极大的机智和细腻。

28
00:14:29.000 --> 00:15:19.000
He said, Teodros is very dedicated to the idea of restoring the glories of the Ethiopian Empire and achieving great conquests. He's man of untiring energy. His personal and moral daring is boundless. Now, there are a couple of flaws that Plowden notes as well. his violent anger at times, his unyielding pride as regards his kingly and divine right, and his fanatical religious zeal. However, Plowden says this is a man we can do business with. He wants to reclaim Abyssinia's ancient borders. He dreams of retaking Egypt for Christianity, marching all the way to Cairo and Alexandria, and then, and I quote, a triumphant march to the holy sepulchre. Can I ask, the foreign office in London wouldn't necessarily approve of these? goals, would they? I think they're mad goals. Because this, of course, is the period when they're
他说，提奥多罗斯非常致力于恢复埃塞俄比亚帝国的荣耀并取得伟大征服的想法。他是一个不知疲倦的人。他个人的和道德上的胆识是无限的。现在，普劳登也注意到了一些缺陷：他有时暴怒，他对自己作为国王和神圣权利的不可动摇的骄傲，以及他狂热的宗教热情。然而，普劳登说这是一个我们可以与之做生意的人。他想收回阿比西尼亚的古老边界。他梦想着为基督教夺回埃及，一路进军开罗和亚历山大，然后，我引用，‘凯旋进军圣墓’。我可以问一下，伦敦的外交部不一定会批准这些目标，对吧？我认为这些是疯狂的目标。因为这当然是他们

29
00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:54.000
starting to think about a Suez Canal. They don't want to offend the Ottomans. No, they don't want to offend the Ottomans particularly with whom they, of course, they're allied in the Crimean War. I mean, British interests in Egypt are extremely important to them. They do not want to alienate the Egyptians, and this, as we'll see, becomes a problem. So on top of that, Britain is Christian power, Ethiopia is the Christian power? Is there any sense that, therefore, they are natural allies? Or is Britain simply, it's all about real politics? It's all about imperial priorities. The Christian element just doesn't really enter the calculations of diplomats in London.
开始考虑苏伊士运河的时期。他们不想冒犯奥斯曼人。不，他们不想冒犯奥斯曼人，特别是他们当然在克里米亚战争中与他们结盟。我的意思是，英国在埃及的利益对他们来说极其重要。他们不想疏远埃及人，正如我们将看到的，这成了一个问题。最重要的是，英国是基督教大国，埃塞俄比亚是基督教大国？有没有一种感觉，因此他们是天然的盟友？还是英国纯粹是现实政治？这完全是关于帝国优先事项。基督教因素并没有真正进入伦敦外交官的计算中。

30
00:15:54.000 --> 00:16:20.000
It's important to some people. It's definitely important to Plowton. It's important, as we will see, to lots of missionaries and missionary organizations. But not to the government. But to the government, I would argue, probably not. I think they certainly have religious prejudices, but there's an awful lot of realpolitik in this, as we will find out. Anyway, Plowden says to Tildros, would you like to some? a treaty with Britain and Teodros hesitates. What he doesn't want to do is sign a treaty that would
